spension and/or termination of the employment relationship with ISU . Job Duties Essential Duties and Responsibilities The student director is responsible for providing nightly astronomical viewing sessions on Tuesday and Friday evenings after sunset. The student director must be familiar with constellations and astronomic objects visible during the fall, winter and spring seasons. The student director is also responsible for traveling to off campus sites to conduct viewing events at area high schools and other venues and for hosting educational viewing events on campus for visiting middle school students and youth programs. The student director must be familiar with the use of reflecting telescopes, must be comfortable talking in front of groups, and must be able to put together multimedia presentations. Engineering abilities to conduct telescope repairs is preferred.. Career Readiness Competencies o\tCritical Thinking/Problem Solving: Exercise sound reasoning to analyze issues, make decisions, and overcome problems. The individual is able to obtain, interpret, and use knowledge, facts, and data in the process, and may demonstrate originality and inventiveness. o\tOral/Written Communications: Articulate thoughts and ideas clearly and effectively in written and oral forms to persons inside and outside of the organization. The individual has public speaking skills; is able to express ideas to others; and can write/edit memos, letters, and complex technical reports clearly and effectively. o\tTeamwork/Collaboration: Build collaborative relationships with colleagues and customers representing diverse cultures, races, ages, genders, religions, lifestyles, and viewpoints. The individual is able to work within a team structure, and can negotiate and manage conflict. o\tDigital Technology: Select and use appropriate technology to accomplish a given task. The individual is also able to apply computing skills to solve problems. o\tLeadership: Leverage the strengths of others